  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 244 AM EST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\n- Light rain possible early today\n\nAn area of precipitation has dissipated as it moved east across \nsouthwest Lower MI. However, some models, such as the HRRR, \nsuggest that another batch of light showers could move northward \ninto extreme southwest Lower MI later this morning, possibly \naffecting that area with another round of light rain. Cloud bases \nremain high and a dry sub-cloud layer, as noted on the 01/00Z DTX \nsounding, will limit precipitation accumulations. We expect \npredominantly dry conditions from this afternoon onward.\n\n\n- Dry conditions into Monday\n\nSatellite shows a sharp upper trough off the west coast with\ndownstream ridging over the intermountain west. This ridge will \nmove eastward and be positioned over the western Great Lakes by \nMonday afternoon, resulting in synoptic subsidence for our region \nup through this time. Based on this, it is not surprising that more\nrecently available ensemble and deterministic NWP suggests Monday\ncould be predominantly dry for southwest Lower MI with precipitation\nholding off until Monday night or Tuesday, while better chances \nremain west and north of the area.\n\n\n- Wetter conditions Tuesday into the weekend\n \nChances for rain grow substantially starting late Tuesday. \nAs a side note regarding Tuesday, highs are expected to reach the\nlower 70s, which is about 20 degrees above normal for this time \nof year. Even Wednesday will be making a run close to 70 degrees.\n\nTotal QPF for the coming week looks a bit less impressive overall.\nFor example, a sampling of available medium range ensemble guidance\n(GEFS/ECE/Canadian) shows only about a 50 percent chance of accumulating\nan inch or precipitation between now and Friday evening.\n\nHowever, there is one important caveat regarding rain potential \nlater this coming week. There is growing consensus that a cutoff \nlow will form in the Wednesday timeframe somewhere over the \nmidwest. ECE mean 500 mb height forecasts show this, which indicates\nsurprisingly good positional and temporal consistency among the \nensemble members. This low develops from the aforementioned upper \nwave, which by this time will be poised to cross the Mississippi \nRiver. During the cutoff process, there is the possibility for \nmid-level deformation and attendant fgen to materialize somewhere\ndownstream of the upper low, which could in turn produce localized\namounts of much greater than an inch of rain towards the latter \npart of the week. It was interesting to observe in the QPF box and\nwhisker plots the presence of significant outliers indicating \nthis possibility. \n\nSo the takeaway is that rainfall amounts for the first part of the\nweek have relatively good predictability with low potential for\noverperforming. The latter half of the week, on the other hand, has\nmuch less predictability with a small chance of significant \nrainfall in a concentrated area.",
